                        "description": "## Vulnerability Details\n  **File Location**: `SKILL.md`, lines 15-27  \n  **Vulnerability Type**: Unpinned dependency execution  \n  **Risk Level**: Medium\n\n  ### Vulnerable Code\n\n  ```markdown\n  ## What are the requirements?\n\n  - `uv` installed (for `uvx`)\n  - `HF_TOKEN` env var or `--hf-token` flag (for gated or private models only)\n\n  ## How to run?\n\n  Run with `--model-id` pointing to the Hugging Face Hub repository which will check that it either contains Safetensors (via `model.safetensors`, `model.safetensors.index.json` if sharded, or `model_index.json` for Diffusers) or GGUF model weights within.\n\n  ```bash\n  uvx hf-mem --model-id &lt;model-id&gt; --json-output\n  ```\n  ```\n\n  Equivalent unpinned `uvx hf-mem` commands also occur throughout lines 34-79.\n\n  ### Technical Analysis\n\n  The Skill instructs the agent to use `uvx` to resolve, download, and execute the third-party `hf-mem` package without specifying an audited version, package hash, lockfile, or verified source. Consequently, the code executed at runtime is not fully represented by the reviewed Skill and can change independently after the audit.\n\n  This creates a software supply-chain exposure. If the package publisher account, package registry, package distribution process, or a future package release is compromised, an attacker-controlled package could execute automatically when the documented command is invoked. The risk is heightened because the documentation identifies `HF_TOKEN` as an environment variable that may be present during execution.\n\n  The reviewed `SKILL.md` itself contains no embedded executable payload, obfuscated code, persistence mechanism, or explicit credential-exfiltration instruction. Exploitation depends on compromise or malicious replacement of the externally resolved dependency.\n\n  ### Attack Path\n\n  1. An attacker compromises the package publisher, registry entry, or distribution channel used to resolve `hf-mem`, or publishes a malicious version through an applicable package-resolution attack.\n  2. The agent follows the Skill and runs `uvx hf-mem` without a pinned version or integrity constraint.\n  3. `uvx` retrieves the currently resolved package rather than a specific previously audited artifact.\n  4. The package executes with the permissions and environment of the agent user.\n  5. Malicious package code can inspect accessible environment variables and files, issue network requests, or execute additional local commands.\n  6. If `HF_TOKEN` is present, malicious code could capture it and use its granted Hugging Face permissions.\n\n  ### Impact Assessment\n\n  Successful exploitation provides code execution under the account running the agent. The attacker could access files readable by that account, inspect environment variables, communicate over the network, and invoke commands available to the process. A present `HF_TOKEN` could be exposed, allowing access within the token's configured scope, potentially including gated or private Hugging Face repositories.\n\n  This finding does not establish privilege escalation beyond the invoking user's permissions. Its practical scope depends on process isolation, filesystem permissions, network controls, the privileges assigned to `HF_TOKEN`, and other credentials available in the execution environment.",
