                      "properties": {
                        "description": "<![CDATA[\n## Vulnerability Details\n\n**File Location**: `SKILL.md`, lines 1120–1123  \n**Vulnerability Type**: Persistent instruction poisoning through mandatory self-modification  \n**Risk Level**: Medium\n\n### Vulnerable Instruction\n\nThe following is a faithful English rendering of the relevant instruction:\n\n```markdown\nWhen you obtain a correct result only after multiple attempts—for example, after\ntrial and error with parameter formats, changing interface selection, or discovering\nundocumented constraints—you must briefly append the experience to the pitfall\nrecords in Chapter 16.\n\nRecording standard:\n- Only record cases that succeeded after two or more attempts.\n```\n\n### Technical Analysis\n\nThe Skill requires the agent to append runtime observations to `SKILL.md`, which is persistent instruction state loaded by future sessions. This creates a write channel from runtime data into trusted Skill instructions.\n\nThe instruction does not require:\n\n- Confirmation that the observed workaround is accurate.\n- Verification against authoritative documentation.\n- Sanitization of content originating from API responses or user input.\n- Human approval before modifying the Skill.\n- Separation of untrusted observations from executable agent instructions.\n- Provenance, integrity protection, or rollback support.\n\nIf an external financial-data source, API response, or user-controlled workflow causes repeated failures and then presents a crafted workaround, the agent may treat that workaround as learned experience and persist it. Later sessions could trust the resulting text as part of the Skill.\n\nThis is classified as memory poisoning rather than session-only instruction hijacking because the prescribed modification is intended to survive the current run and influence future sessions.\n\n### Attack Path\n\n1. The agent performs a financial-data query using the Skill.\n2. An attacker-controlled or compromised data source causes the first two or more attempts to fail.\n3. The source then returns a crafted response or workaround that appears successful.\n4. The agent interprets the outcome as a newly discovered constraint or pitfall.\n5. Following the mandatory maintenance instruction, the agent appends the attacker-influenced observation to Chapter 16 of `SKILL.md`.\n6. Future sessions load the modified Skill and may follow the poisoned rule without revalidating its origin or accuracy.\n\nSuccessful exploitation requires the agent process to have write access to the Skill file. The audited project does not itself establish those permissions.\n\n### Impact Assessment\n\nThe issue does not directly grant operating-system privileges or demonstrate arbitrary code execution. Its scope is persistent manipulation of the Skill's future behavior.\n\nPotential consequences include:\n\n- Persistent selection of an attacker-preferred API or data source.\n- Repeated use of incorrect parameters, conversion factors, or fallback rules.\n- Corruption of future financial calculations and reports.\n- Propagation of attacker-influenced instructions across subsequent sessions.\n- Expansion into more serious behavior if a persisted observation instructs future agents to access unsafe resources.\n\nThe maximum demonstrated privilege is modification of files already writable by the agent. No privilege escalation beyond the agent's existing filesystem permissions is established.\n  ]]>",
